We will start with **direct substitution** (step A):

$\begin{align}
g(\blueD{-9})&=\dfrac{(\blueD{-9})+9}{\sqrt{-4(\blueD{-9})}-6}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{0}{\sqrt{36}-6}
\\\\
&=\dfrac00
\end{align}$

We got the **indeterminate form** $\dfrac00$ (step D).

Now we need to determine which analytical method is applicable.

Since we have a quotient with a square root, we will use the method of rationalization by **conjugates** (step F):

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{x+9}{\sqrt{-4x}-6}&=\dfrac{x+9}{\sqrt{-4x}-6}\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt{-4x}+6}{\sqrt{-4x}+6}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{(x+9)(\sqrt{-4x}+6)}{-4x-36}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\cancel{(x+9)}(\sqrt{-4x}+6)}{-4\cancel{(x+9)}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\sqrt{-4x}+6}{-4}
\end{align}$

Now we can try **direct substitution** (step A) once again!

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{\sqrt{-4(\blueD{-9})}+6}{-4}&=\dfrac{\sqrt{36}+6}{-4}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{6+6}{-4}
\\\\
&=-3
\end{align}$

We got a number, so we've **found the limit** (step C)!

This means $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -9}g(x)=-3$.
